(7298784, 1137677, 83149, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Mike:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by damjanev:</b></div><hr/><i> Even with the description and the original shot this looks alike a legit edit in "Advanced". </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>since when are we allowed to remove image area in advanced editing? maybe the shadow would be considered legal since it was shadow, but i''ve seen dq''s for burning down an area so it was obscured even if it was edge of the image.<br/> <br/>i figured removing visible features would be a no-no. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>From the rules:<br/> <br/><b>You May:<br/>   - clone out incidental power lines, twigs, dust specks, stray hairs, and similar minor imperfections within any capture used.<br/></b> <br/>I am pretty sure that covers the wire that was in the original.</td>', 'Originally posted by Mike: Originally posted by damjanev: Even with the description and the original shot this looks alike a legit edit in "Advanced".   since when are we allowed to remove image area in advanced editing? maybe the shadow would be considered legal since it was shadow, but i''ve seen dq''s for burning down an area so it was obscured even if it was edge of the image. i figured removing visible features would be a no-no.   From the rules: You May:   - clone out incidental power lines, twigs, dust specks, stray hairs, and similar minor imperfections within any capture used. I am pretty sure that covers the wire that was in the original.', '2015-01-04 23:03:27.000000', NULL, 0),
